ute a distinct core force beyond T.\",\"evidence\":[{\"block_id\":\"target\",\"quotation\":\"I love your majesty\"}]}},\"original_speech_situation\":\"In a public division of the kingdom, Lear asks Cordelia to say how much she loves him in order to obtain a richer portion. After initially answering “Nothing,” she gives this restrained avowal, contrasting with her sisters’ expansive declarations and measuring her love according to her filial bond.\",\"affection_evidence\":\"present\",\"undertaking_and_uptake\":\"The focal expression makes no undertaking; it avows existing filial love. Cordelia’s surrounding speech invokes already-existing duties to Lear rather than promising a new course of action. Lear does not accept her answer as adequate: he tells her to “Mend your speech a little” and subsequently repudiates her, but that rejection concerns the sufficiency of her declaration, not uptake of a promise.\",\"recognised_work\":true,\"recognition_note\":\"The supplied text is recognisable as William Shakespeare’s King Lear, Act I, Scene I.\",\"limitations\":\"The classification is restricted to the marked occurrence and supplied passages. The evidence strongly supports sincere affection, including Cordelia’s aside that her love is “More ponderous than my tongue” and her later conduct, but sincerity is recorded separately from the utterance’s T force.\"}"
